{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"general-purpose","blocks":[{"text":"# Report: conversation-ledger docs review\n\nSources read: `/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md`, `/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md`, `/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Product intent and core guarantees\n\n**README intro:** \"Durable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in your repository as git notes.\" It \"appends immutable JSONL evidence events to `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the conversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its conversations come along — never touch your working tree or `git status`.\" And: \"The ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was visibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\"\n\n**PRODUCT_INTENT.md \"Purpose\":** \"Conversation Ledger preserves durable, inspectable records of visible conversations between people and coding agents. It is a general conversation provenance primitive, not an intent-analysis product. Downstream consumers may use its records as evidence about why a repository changed, but the ledger must not impose that interpretation.\" The record must remain useful beyond a single tool: \"cross-CLI continuity, archival/export, code attribution, review, and later intent retrieval are all valid independent uses.\"\n\n**\"Auditable history\" here means:**\n- Events are immutable and content-addressed: `id` is `ev1-` + sha256 of the identity subset, so \"capture is idempotent by construction, and the id doubles as the content hash that makes mutation detectable\" (TECHNICAL_DESIGN, \"Canonical objects\").\n- \"Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently rewriting history\" (README, \"Storage model\"); TECHNICAL_DESIGN calls this \"honest history without secret retention.\"\n- Behavioral commitments (PRODUCT_INTENT): \"Preserve source material and provenance; do not silently rewrite history\"; \"Preserve exact visible turns before creating optional derived views\"; record \"repository, branch, commit, worktree state, author, timestamps, and capture mechanism with each artifact whenever available\"; \"Keep storage out of ordinary working-tree status views while retaining normal Git durability and shareability\"; \"Make sharing, retention, redaction, and deletion deliberate user choices.\"\n\n**Non-goals** (PRODUCT_INTENT): no deciding which statement is correct/intended; no summarizing/ranking/embedding/interpreting; no agent control or workflow enforcement; no capturing provider-hidden reasoning. \"Those belong to clients such as Turnbridge and Intent Recall, not to this core storage layer.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. Full roadmap\n\n**README \"Roadmap\" section** (verbatim item names, condensed bodies):\n\n1. **Path-based capture exclusion** — \"the path half of the redaction config ('never record reads of `secrets/**`'); requires correlating `tool_use` file paths with their `tool_result` events.\" Not done.\n2. **Post-push purge** — \"true content removal after the ledger ref has been shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`.\" Not done (only the local pre-push half shipped).\n3. **`re-anchor`** — \"reattach events orphaned by squash merges or history rewrites. The desired end state is that this happens by default: a squash commit should end up carrying the conversations of the branch it squashed. Hard part: squashes often happen off-machine (GitHub 'Squash and merge'), so detection needs patch-id/branch-tip matching after fetch, not a local hook.\" Not done.\n4. **Harness-artifact capture** — \"decide whether the ledger should also preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die with a worktree 