{"actor":{"id":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"Explore","blocks":[{"text":"## Report: sticky-note Repository Analysis\n\n### 1. What It Is & Purpose\n\n**Sticky Note** is a git-backed **team handoff system for AI coding assistants** (Claude Code, Copilot CLI, Codex). It solves a critical coordination problem: when multiple developers use AI assistants, each agent works in isolation, duplicating effort and losing context between sessions. Sticky Note captures session threads (files touched, narrative, failed approaches, conversation prompts, status) and surfaces relevant prior work to teammates automatically.\n\n**Core tagline:** \"Human-to-human handoff for AI coding assistants\" — metadata and context sharing between coding sessions across a team.\n\n---\n\n### 2. How It Stores Data\n\n**Primary storage:** `.git/sticky-note/` (inside the `.git` directory, branch-independent)\n\n- **sticky-note.json** — Central thread ledger with per-thread metadata: status (open/stuck/stale/closed/expired), author, files touched, narrative, failed approaches, work type, conversation prompts, resume chain, contributor list\n- **audit/<username>.jsonl** — Per-user JSONL audit trail (one JSON object per line) tracking tool usage, files modified, session IDs\n- **presence/<username>.json** — Per-user presence files showing who's active and what files they're working on\n- **Git Notes storage** — Session attribution stored in `refs/notes/sticky-note` for line-level git blame attribution (survives rebase/amend)\n\n**Advanced design (v3.0.0):** Uses a dedicated orphan branch **`sticky-note/data`** to hold all thread metadata separate from feature branches. Eliminates two critical failure modes: branch time-travel (old .sticky-note.json on branch checkout) and write-race corruption (file truncation during pre-commit hook on Windows). Data syncs at session start (fetch) and end (commit+push to the data branch).\n\n**Merge strategy:** Custom git merge driver (`merge=sticky-note`) performs field-level 3-way merge on threads by ID rather than line-based union, handling concurrent edits intelligently.\n\n---\n\n### 3. CLI/API Surface & Design Philosophy\n\n**Unix-style composable philosophy:** Sticky Note follows minimal CLI design with powerful read/query commands, no destructive defaults, and composable hooks into the AI assistant workflow.\n\n**Main commands:**\n- `init` — Interactive setup (installs hooks, templates, data branch)\n- `threads` / `audit` / `who` — Read-only queries (list threads, filter by user/file/time, show presence)\n- `resume <id>` / `resume-thread --query \"...\"` — Thread handoff across tools\n- `overlap` / `claim` — Explicit conflict detection before editing\n- `sync` — Manual commit/push of `.git/sticky-note/` to remote\n- `status` / `gc` / `reset` — Diagnostics and cleanup\n- `get-line-attribution --file <path>` — Map file lines to originating threads\n- `mcp-server` — Exposes 8-tool MCP server (get_session_context, search_threads, check_overlaps, get_audit_trail, etc.)\n\n**Hook architecture:** Five collection hooks + one injection hook + one pre-tool-use hook:\n- **Collectors:** `track-work.js` (audit on tool use), `session-end.js` (capture thread on exit), `on-error.js` (mark stuck), `on-stop.js` (handoff summary)\n- **Injectors:** `session-start.js` (eager injection at boot), `inject-context.js` (relevance scoring on each prompt), `pre-tool-use.js` (lazy git-blame-driven injection)\n\n**Design principles:**\n- **No code capture** — Only metadata (files, timestamps, narrative summaries, error patterns)\n- **Git-native** — No external DB; all data versioned in git; merge driver handles concurrent teams\n- **Offline-safe** — Local cache in `.git/sticky-note/`, syncs when connectivity available\n- **Tool-agnostic** — Same hook scripts work across Claude Code, Copilot CLI, Codex; MCP server for third-party tools\n- **Unix philosophy** — Small, single-purpose commands; composable; reads from files; no hidden state\n\n---\n\n### 4. Maturity Level\n\n**Production-ready with sophisticated architecture:**\n- **Version 3.0.0** (released) with major v3 redesign (data-branch storage)\n- **Working codebase** in Node.js 16+, not design docs; active development (commits through 2026-07)\n- **~170MB repo** with comprehensive documentation, design specs, templates, example demo threads\n- **Test coverage** — Smoke tests in `test/smoke.test.js` (CLI command non-crash verification)\n- **Public on npm** — `sticky-note-cli` available via `npx`; used in production by teams\n- **Maintained** — Recent refactors, bug fixes (write-race fix, stale hook path detection), v3.0 migration support\n- **Rich docs** — README (23KB), CLAUDE.md (10KB), 10+ design/feature docs (branch-switching, MCP server, thread resume, smart injection, etc.)\n- **Change log** — CHANGELOG.md with per-version feature/fix breakdown\n\n---\n\n### 5. Relevance to conversation-ledger\n\n**Strong structural parallels; sticky-note is a template for how to build conversation-ledger:**\n\n| Aspect | sticky-note | conversation-ledger (planned) |\n|--------|------------|------------------------------|\n| **Core data** | Session threads (AI context metadata) | Conversation transcripts (AI→human handoff) |\n| **Storage** | Git-backed `.git/sticky-note/` branch | Git-backed append-only store (you're designing) |\n| **Distribution** | Team shared via git; no external DB | Solo/team shared via git; append-only |\n| **Interface** | CLI + hooks + MCP server | CLI + git plumbing (you're building) |\n| **Philosophy** | Unix-composition; metadata only | Unix-composition; transcript log format |\n| **Conflict resolution** | Custom merge driver (3-way field-merge) | Append-only negates conflicts |\n| **Offline-first** | Local cache, sync on session end | Local first; push on demand |\n\n**Why sticky-note is relevant:**\n\n1. **Prior art on git-backed metadata storage** — Shows how to store semantic data (not just source code) in git without triggering merge conflicts or write races. The data-branch design elegantly separates concerns.\n\n2. **Hook-driven architecture** — Demonstrates inserting capture logic into AI tool workflows without modifying the tools themselves. Could inform conversation-ledger's hook/signal story.\n\n3. **Relevance scoring & injection** — Three-tier attribution (git notes → audit JSONL → file+date heuristic) is sophisticated pattern for \"what context is relevant to load?\" — applicable to finding relevant prior conversations.\n\n4. **MCP server** — Shows how to expose git-backed data as structured tools for AI reasoning; conversation-ledger might follow similar MCP or CLI pattern.\n\n5. **Team conflict handling** — Field-level merge driver handles concurrent pushes. Append-only stores avoid this, but the architecture patterns (branch-independent cache, fetch-before-push protocol) are reusable.\n\n**Recommendation:** Study sticky-note's `data-branch.js`, merge driver, and session-start/end sync protocol as templates. Conversation-ledger's simpler append-only model means simpler locking/merging, but the git plumbing, offline-first design, and hook integration patterns are directly applicable.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:700cd82f-2f79-4cd6-b727-0f81230e0837#agent:af619167936cb352b","parent":"claude-code:700cd82f-2f79-4cd6-b727-0f81230e0837","seq":55},"id":"ev1-785162a543fb7232c15ff8cb5e5e03957494a3ef144a0776864b385840d00f9a","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-18T22:14:06.339Z","producer":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","session_id":"700cd82f-2f79-4cd6-b727-0f81230e0837","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.214","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"af619167936cb352b","attr