{"actor":{"id":"claude-sonnet-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"general-purpose","blocks":[{"text":"## Findings\n\n**1. HIGH — Capture-time secret redaction runs on the reasoning ciphertext and can silently corrupt it, unconditionally.**\n`appendEvents` (`src/store.ts:177-184`) calls `redactDraft` on **every** draft's `raw.data`, including the new `reasoningDraft` output (`src/adapters/drift.ts:70-106`), with no kind-based exemption. `redactDraft`/`walkStrings` (`src/redact/apply.ts:100-148`) deep-walks string leaves and blind-replaces any capture-tier regex match (github/openai/aws/slack/jwt/etc., `src/redact/rules.ts:27-107`) with a `[REDACTED:...]` placeholder — including inside `encrypted_content`. Capture-tier redaction is **on by default** (`captureRules`, `src/redact/config.ts:101-104`, only off if `redact.capture === false`).\n\nI reproduced this directly against the built code: feeding a synthetic `reasoning` draft whose `raw.data.payload.encrypted_content` merely *contains* a `ghp_`-shaped substring through `redactDraft(rules=capture)` mutates the stored blob (`AAAA/ghp_aaa…/BBBB` → `AAAA/[REDACTED:github-token:ba94fef94606]/BBBB`). Since real Codex ciphertext blobs are ~2-4KB of opaque base64 (per the roadmap's own estimate), a coincidental match against one of ~12 capture-tier patterns over enough turns is a real, non-hypothetical risk. This is undetectable from the outside — a reasoning event's `redactions` array looks identical whether it hit a real secret or an unlucky ciphertext byte run — and it permanently breaks the one thing this feature exists for (byte-exact replay through the provider's API). No test in the repo exercises this path (`grep -rn reasoning src/test/*.ts` outside `adapter-codex.test.ts` returns nothing).\n\n**2. MEDIUM — `cledger scan --paranoid` will flag essentially every reasoning event, and the findings can never be durably allowlisted.**\n`scanEvents` (`src/redact/scan.ts:67-104`) walks `event.raw.data` for all events with no kind exemption. The paranoid tier's blanket rule (`src/redact/rules.ts:146-154`, `/\\b[A-Za-z0-9+/_=-]{24,}\\b/`, entropy ≥ 4.0) matches base64 ciphertext essentially always. Each blob differs per turn, so its fingerprint (`sha256Hex(matchText)`) differs every time too — `cledger allow` can't suppress the class, only one already-seen blob. This is the exact scenario the pre-edit roadmap text explicitly said the design would avoid (\"excluded from … secret scans, which cannot scan ciphertext anyway\" — removed in this diff's README hunk without being replaced by an equivalent guarantee or code).\n\n**3. LOW — `cmdConversations` doesn't hide `reasoning` events like `log`/`show` do.** `src/cli.ts:238-258` has no reasoning filter, so per-conversation counts include opaque blobs and there's no `--with-reasoning` symmetry/opt-out there.\n\n**Verified clean:** `npm test` → 131 pass, 1 skip (gitleaks oracle unavailable), 0 fail, build succeeds. `renormalize.ts` correctly never touches `reasoning`-kind events (filters strictly on `kind === \"unrecognized\"`). USAGE text, flag parsing, and `--with-reasoning` threading in `cli.ts` are internally consistent — no typos or mismatches found there.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:ca50d7db-8621-495c-a94a-c3d3ab731073#agent:aed5fef0c20867100","parent":"claude-code:ca50d7db-8621-495c-a94a-c3d3ab731073","seq":58},"id":"ev1-792fb53b7f188016c3a77294dc7e4561b9ac2cd14f80eecf44ae5b506e11aee8","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-22T21:24:40.662Z","producer":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","session_id":"ca50d7db-8621-495c-a94a-c3d3ab731073","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.217","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"aed5fef0c20867100","attributionAgent":"g