{"actor":{"id":"claude-sonnet-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"general-purpose","blocks":[{"text":"## Question 1 — GUI wrappers sharing CLI backends\n\n**OpenAI Codex.** Confirmed: Codex CLI/TUI/exec, the Desktop (macOS) app, and the IDE extension all read the same `~/.codex/config.toml` layers and write to the same `~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-*.jsonl` format ([config reference](https://developers.openai.com/codex/config-reference), [hooks docs](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks)). The hooks engine (`[hooks]` in config.toml or `hooks.json`) is confirmed shared between CLI and Desktop — a regression that broke hooks in both simultaneously after a Desktop update is strong evidence of one shared engine ([issue #21639](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/21639)). However, this sharing is buggy in practice: the Desktop app has open bugs where it fails to load the user-level config.toml ([#25145](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/25145)) or ignores project-local `.codex/config.toml` ([#13025](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/13025)).\n\nFor the **IDE extension (VS Code/JetBrains)**: config.toml layers are shared, but a third-party source states plainly that \"the experimental hooks engine fires in CLI sessions but not IDE sessions\" ([codex.danielvaughan.com](https://codex.danielvaughan.com/2026/04/01/codex-ide-extension-vs-code-jetbrains/), not an official OpenAI source — treat as inference/unconfirmed by OpenAI itself). Whether it writes local rollout files wasn't explicitly confirmed either way.\n\nFor **Codex Cloud/web tasks**: these run in OpenAI-hosted containers, disconnected from your machine by default ([codex cloud docs](https://developers.openai.com/codex/cloud)) — so they do not produce local rollout files during execution; state lives server-side. A third-party source claims \"CLI, Desktop app, IDE extension and Cloud runtime all sharing a unified backend\" making threads resumable across surfaces, but this is not an official confirmation.\n\n**Claude Code.** Confirmed via official docs ([desktop docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop), [vs-code docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/vs-code)):\n- **Desktop app (Mac/Windows)**: \"The desktop app reads the same settings files as the CLI.\" Local/SSH/WSL sessions run on your machine and behave like the CLI (hooks + `~/.claude/projects` transcripts implied, not 100% explicitly spelled out for transcript writing but strongly implied by shared-settings language and local execution model). Its separate **Cowork** tab is a different product that explicitly does *not* fire `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks ([issue #63360](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/63360)). \"Remote\" (cloud) sessions run on Anthropic's infrastructure, not locally.\n- **VS Code extension**: explicitly documented — \"Claude Code settings in `~/.claude/settings.json`: shared between the extension and CLI. Use for ... hooks...\"; extension and CLI \"share the same conversation history,\" with a Local/Remote session-history picker.\n- **JetBrains**: it's a wrapper that runs the actual `claude` CLI in the integrated terminal, so it's literally the CLI (same hooks/transcripts).\n- **claude.ai/code (web)**: used to monitor/continue cloud (\"Remote\") sessions, which run on Anthropic's servers, not your machine — no local transcript/hooks there.\n\n**Cursor**: has a documented hooks system ([cursor.com/docs/hooks](https://cursor.com/docs/hooks)) with lifecycle events (`beforeShellExecution`, `afterFileEdit`, `sessionStart`/`sessionEnd`, etc.), though cloud agents lack MCP and session-lifecycle hooks ([gitbutler.com](https://blog.gitbutler.com/cursor-hooks-deep-dive)). Local storage is split: IDE chats live in editor-internal storage (`state.vscdb`), while `cursor-agent` CLI sessions live under `~/.cursor/chats` ([vibe-replay.com](https://vibe-replay.com/blog/cursor-local-storage/)).\n\n## Question 2 — other CLIs\n\n- **Kimi CLI/Kimi Code (Moonshot)**: session history/logs under `~/.kimi-code/`; has a lifecycle hooks system \"inspired by Claude Code\" (SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.) ([kimi-cli #785](https://github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-cli/issues/785)).\n- **opencode (sst)**: local data under `~/.local/share/opencode/`, now SQLite-backed (migrated from per-session JSON); plugin API (`@opencode-ai/plugin`) provides event hooks ([deepwiki](https://deepwiki.com/sst/opencode/2.9-storage-and-database)).\n- **Gemini CLI**: chats/checkpoints as JSON under `~/.gemini/tmp/<project_hash>/{chats,checkpoints}/`; documented hooks in `settings.json` ([geminicli.com/docs/hooks](https://geminicli.com/docs/hooks/)).\n- **aider**: plain-text `.aider.chat.history.md` in the project dir; no dedicated hook/lifecycle-event engine found — could not confirm one exists.\n- **GitHub Copilot CLI**: session files under `~/.copilot/session-state/` plus a local SQLite session store; documented hooks (`~/.copilot/hooks/`, repo `.github/hooks/`) ([GitHub docs](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-cli/customize-copilot/use-hooks)).\n- **Amp (Sourcegraph)**: threads sync to Sourcegraph's cloud (`ampcode.com/threads`) by default, not local-first; JS/TS plugin API with lifecycle events (`session.start`, `tool.call`) rather than shell-hook files ([ampcode.com/manual](https://ampcode.com/manual)).\n- **Factory droid**: sessions under `~/.factory/`; hooks in `~/.factory/hooks.json` ([docs.factory.ai](https://docs.factory.ai/cli/configuration/hooks-guide)).\n- **Qwen Code**: chats under `~/.qwen/projects/<project>/chats/`; hooks system with recent HTTP/Function/Async hook types ([qwen-code hooks](https://qwenlm.github.io/qwen-code-docs/en/users/features/hooks/)).\n- **Goose (Block)**: sessions in a SQLite DB at `{data_dir}/sessions/sessions.db` (migrated from `.jsonl` at v1.10.0); hooks support is unclear/contested — one sandbox analysis found no lifecycle-hook system, so treat as unconfirmed.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"conte