;\n  hook_event_name?: string;\n  turn_id?: string;\n}\n```\n\n#### b) **Transcript line interface**\nDefines a local interface for parsing one line of the native transcript format:\n```ts\n// claude-code.ts\ninterface ClaudeTranscriptLine {\n  type?: string; isSidechain?: boolean; message?: { role?: string; model?: string; content?: unknown };\n  uuid?: string; parentUuid?: string; sessionId?: string; timestamp?: string;\n  cwd?: string; gitBranch?: string; version?: string;\n}\n// codex.ts\ninterface CodexRolloutLine {\n  type?: string; timestamp?: string; payload?: Record<string, unknown>;\n}\n```\n\n#### c) **Constants**\n- `RAW_FORMAT` — a versioned format string (e.g. `\"claude-code-jsonl/1\"`, `\"codex-rollout-jsonl/2\"`)\n- `CONVERTIBLE_LINE_TYPES` — Set of line types the adapter can interpret as conversation turns\n- `KNOWN_SKIPPED_LINE_TYPES` — Set of line types deliberately skipped (bookkeeping, UI state, etc.)\n\n#### d) **Core functions**\n1. **`packageVersion()`** — reads cledger's own version from `package.json`\n2. **`sanitizeId()`** — sanitizes session IDs for use as filenames\n3. **`cursorPath/repo`** — path to cursor tracking file under `repo.commonDir/conversation-ledger/cursors/`\n4. **`readCursor/writeCursor`** — track how many lines have been read from a transcript file (incremental capture)\n5. **`convertLine()`** — the main conversion function: takes a parsed transcript line + metadata, returns `EventDraft | null`\n6. **`preserve()`** — creates an `unrecognized` event for lines the adapter cannot interpret (via `drift.ts`)\n7. **`renormalizeUnrecognized()`** — re-normalization hook: takes a stored `unrecognized` event and re-interprets it as a `conversation_turn` (same id as live capture would produce)\n\n#### e) **Hook entrypoint**\n```ts\nexport async function runClaudeCodeHook(stdinJson: string): Promise<void>\nexport async function runCodexHook(stdinJson: string): Promise<void>\n```\n- Parses hook payload, extracts cwd and transcript_path\n- Calls `findRepo()` — silently returns if not in a git repo (hooks must never break sessions)\n- Calls the capture function\n\n#### f) **Capture function**\n```ts\nexport async function captureClaudeTranscript(transcriptPath: string, cwd: string): Promise<CaptureResult>\nexport async function captureCodexTranscript(transcriptPath: string, cwd: string): Promise<CaptureResult>\n```\n- Returns `CaptureResult` from `drift.ts`: `{ appended, deduped, unrecognized }`\n- Reads the transcript file\n- Splits into lines, reads cursor to resume from where it left off\n- For each line: parses JSON, classifies by type, converts to draft or preserves as unrecognized\n- Calls `appendEvents()` from store to write to git notes\n- Updates cursor position\n\n### `drift.ts` — Shared utilities\n\nExports:\n- **`CaptureResult`** interface: `{ appended: number; deduped: number; unrecognized: Record<string, number> }`\n- **`countUnrecognized()`** — tallies unrecognized line types for drift warning\n- **`unrecognizedDraft()`** — builds a raw-only preservation `EventDraft` with kind `\"unrecognized\"`\n- **`reasoningDraft()`** — builds an opaque `\"reasoning\"` event for provider-encrypted content\n- **`warnUnrecognized()`** — prints warning about unrecognized lines\n\n### Agent Context Pattern (ProducerAgentContext)\n\nBoth adapters gather agent facts from the source transcript:\n- **Claude Code**: stamps `version` on every line, `model` on assistant lines → `agentContext()` extracts them per-line\n- **Codex**: uses `session_meta` / `turn_context` lines that precede response items → rolling `CodexAgentState` with `applyContextLine()` and `initialAgentContext()` (which re-reads file prefix for resume correctness)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core Schema (`src/schema.ts`)\n\n### Key Types\n\n```ts\nexport const SCHEMA_VERSION = \"conversation-ledger/v1\";\n\n// Event kinds (open string, but known ones):\nconst KNOWN_KINDS = [\n  \"conversation_turn\", \"decision\", \"document\", \"annotation\",\n  \"redaction\", \"supersession\", \"re_anchor\", \"unrecognized\", \"reasoning\"\n];\n\ninterface Actor { type: string; id?: string; display?: string; }\ninterface Producer { tool: string; version?: string; source?: string; source_version?: string; model?: string; provider?: string; session_id?: string; }\ntype ProducerAgentContext = Pick<Producer, \"source_version\" | \"model\" | \"provider\">;\ninterface ConversationRef { id: string; seq: number; }\ninterface EvidenceEvent { id: string; schema: string; kind: string; occurred_at: string; recorded_at: string; actor: Actor; producer: Producer; content: unknown; context?: RepoContext; conversation?: ConversationRef; links?: EventLink[]; raw?: { format: string; data: unknown }; redactions?: RedactionRecord[]; }\ntype EventDraft = Omit<EvidenceEvent, \"id\" | \"schema\" | \"recorded_at\"> & Partial<Pick<EvidenceEvent, \"id\" | \"schema\" | \"recorded_at\">>;\n```\n\n### Event Identity\n- `eventId()`: SHA256 of canonical JSON of identity subset: `{ schema, kind, occurred_at, actor.type+id, producer.source+session_id, conversation, media_type, content, links }`\n- Notably **excludes**: `recorded_at`, `context`, `raw`, `producer.version/tool/model/provider/source_version`\n- This ensures re-scanning the same source always produces the same ID (idempotent capture)\n\n### Event Finalization\n- `finalizeEvent()`: fills id, schema, recorded_at; validates; returns `EvidenceEvent`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Configuration Files\n\n### `package.json`\n- ESM (`\"type\": \"module\"`)\n- Bin: `conversation-ledger` and `cledger` -> `dist/cli.js`\n- Main: `dist/index.js` (library entry point)\n- Scripts: `build` (tsc + chmod), `test` (build then `node --test`)\n- No runtime deps, only `typescript` + `@types/node` dev deps\n\n### `tsconfig.json`\n- Target: ES2022, module: NodeNext, strict mode on\n- `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `declaration`, `sourceMap`\n- Root: `src/`, out: `dist/`\n\n### `.gitignore`\n- Standard: `.DS_Store`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `coverage/`, `.env`\n\n---\n\n## 5. OpenCode References\n\nFound **1 reference** to \"opencode\" in the codebase:\n\n**File:** `README.md`, **Line 152:**\n> **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n\nThis is in the TODO adapters list — **no adapter implementation for opencode exists yet**. It is listed as a future/additional adapter alongside Gemini CLI, Kimi CLI, GitHub Copilot CLI, Factory droid, Qwen Code, Cursor, aider, and Goose/Amp.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Adapter Conventions Summary\n\nWhen creating a **new adapter** (e.g. for opencode), you would follow this pattern:\n\n| Aspect | Convention |\n|---|---|\n| **File location** | `src/adapters/<name>.ts` |\n| **Raw format string** | `\"<source>-<format>/<version>\"` (e.g. `\"claude-code-jsonl/1\"`) |\n| **Hook payload interface** | Define a local interface for the CLI's hook stdin |\n| **Transcript line interface** | Define a local interface for parsing native transcript lines |\n| **Convertible types** | `Set<string>` of line types that become `conversation_turn` events |\n| **Known skipped types** | `Set<string>` of line types deliberately not captured |\n| **Cursor tracking** | `cursorPath(repo, sessionId)`, `readCursor()`, `writeCursor()` under `repo.commonDir/conversation-ledger/cursors/` |\n| **`convertLine()`** | Maps a parsed line -> `EventDraft` or null (unrecognized) |\n| **`preserve()`** | Calls `unrecognizedDraft()` from `drift.ts` for unrecognized types |\n| **`renormalizeUnrecognized()`** | Exported for `renormalize.ts` — re-interprets stored unrecognized events |\n| **`run<Source>Hook()`** | Entrypoint called from CLI `hook` subcommand |\n| **`capture<Source>Transcript()`** | Main capture logic: read file, parse lines, convert, append, update cursor |\n| **Agent context** | Gather `ProducerAgentContext` (model, provider, source_version) from wherever the source records it |\n| **Imports** | `findRepo`, `gitUserIdentity` from `../git.js`; `appendEvents` from `../store.js`; types from `../schema.js`; `unrecognizedDraft`, `reasoningDraft`, `CaptureResult` from `./drift.js` |\n| **Error handling** | Hook functions silently return if not in a repo; capture functions throw if no repo |\n| **Output** | Capture functions write to stderr: `\"cledger: <source> +N events (M deduped)\"` |\n\n### Required Exports for Library Surface\nThe new adapter's capture + hook functions would need to be exported from `src/index.ts` alongside the existing ones:\n```ts\nexport { captureClaudeTranscript, runClaudeCodeHook } from \"./adapters/claude-code.js\";\nexport { captureCodexTranscript, runCodexHook } from \"./adapters/codex.js\";\n```\n\n### CLI Integration\nThe `cli.ts` `\"hook\"` and `\"capture\"` command handlers would need a new case:\n```ts\ncase \"hook\": {\n  if (positional[0] === \"claude-code\") return runClaudeCodeHook(...);\n  if (positional[0] === \"codex\") return runCodexHook(...);\n  if (positional[0] === \"opencode\") return runOpenCodeHook(...);  // NEW\n}\ncase \"capture\": {\n  if (source === \"claude-code\" && transcript) ...\n  if (source === \"codex\" && transcript) ...\n  if (source === \"opencode\" && transcript) ...  // NEW\n}\n```\n\n### Installation\nThe `install.ts` would need a new `installOpenCode()` function and registration in `installAdapters()`.\n</task_result>\n</task>","status":"completed","time":{"end":1785585754741,"start":1785583231241},"title":"Explore codebase structure"},"tool":"task","type":"tool"}},"format":"opencode-export-json/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T11:06:40.579Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"id":"deepseek-v4-flash","type":"agent"},"content":{"agent":"build","blocks":[{"id":"call_d8a33a9ae5f19fe432a21cd0f1792ab3","input":{"filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md"},"name":"read","type":"tool_use"},{"content":"<path>/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Conversation Ledger\n2: \n3: Durable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4: your repository as git notes.\n5: \n6: `cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7: utility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8: `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9: conversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10: conversations come along, and squash-merged branches are re-anchored onto the\n11: squash commit — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n12: ride your normal `git push`/`git fetch` via an auto-installed, scan-gated\n13: pre-push hook and fetch refspec (or explicitly, via `cledger sync`).\n14: \n15: Conversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n16: \n17: - `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n18: - `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n19: - `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n20: \n21: The ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n22: visibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n23: \n24: ## Install\n25: \n26: ```sh\n27: git clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n28: cd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n29: cledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n30: ```\n31: \n32: `cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n33: and a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n34: backing both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n35: repository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n36: idempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n37: duplicates.\n38: \n39: Two one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n40: capture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n41: interactively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n42: codex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n43: <source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n44: \n45: The hooks above are installed once, globally, and fire in every git\n46: repository you work in. To turn cledger off for one specific repo, add\n47: `{\"enabled\": false}` to that repo's `.cledger.json`: hook capture, manual\n48: `cledger append`, and backfill all become no-ops, before any ledger read or\n49: write happens. Existing history is untouched and still readable via\n50: `cledger log`/`show`/`export` — this only stops new events from being\n51: written. Outside any git repository, hooks are already a silent no-op, so\n52: there's nothing to record and nothing to disable.\n53: \n54: ## Use\n55: \n56: ```sh\n57: cledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n58: cledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n59: cledger log --model gpt-5.6-sol # only turns a given model served\n60: cledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch, with the models they used\n61: cledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n62: cledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n63: cledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n64: cledger re-anchor               # what did the remote squash away? (--apply to map it)\n65: echo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n66: ```\n67: \n68: ## Sharing (transport)\n69: \n70: The ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n71: \n72: - **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n73:   safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n74:   `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n75:   ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n76:   ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n77:   `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n78: - **Push is branch-scoped** (0.14.0): only conversations reachable from the\n79:   refs you're pushing are shared, so an abandoned or unmerged branch's\n80:   conversations stay on your machine. Squash-merged work still rides along —\n81:   the scope follows `re_anchor` mappings the same way reads do.\n82:   `cledger sync --all` pushes the whole ledger; `--rev R` scopes elsewhere.\n83: - **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n84:   `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n85:   (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n86:   `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n87: - **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n88:   `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n89:   marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n90:   explicitly either way.\n91: \n92: If git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n93: are recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n94: the same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n95: and never guesses a hostname-based one.\n96: \n97: ## Squash merges & history rewrites (re-anchoring)\n98: \n99: Events anchor to commits, and forges rewrite commits: a GitHub \"Squash and\n100: merge\" (or \"Rebase and merge\", or a bot amending your push) replaces your\n101: branch's commits with new SHAs, which would orphan their conversations from\n102: the default branch's view. cledger repairs this automatically:\n103: \n104: - After a fetch shows the remote default branch moved, any read compares\n105:   your local branches against it. A branch whose changes match a new commit\n106:   exactly — same tree, or same `git patch-id` fingerprint (squashes as one\n107:   cumulative diff, rebases commit-by-commit) — gets a `re_anchor` mapping\n108:   event, anchored to the surviving commit. `cledger log` then shows the\n109:   branch's conversations as part of the squash commit's history. Notes are\n110:   never moved or rewritten; the mapping is an ordinary append-only event,\n111:   and the original anchor stays in the record as provenance.\n112: - Only exact matches auto-apply. If a maintainer edited during the merge or\n113:   the change matches two commits, nothing is guessed: `cledger re-anchor`\n114:   (dry-run by default, `--apply` to act) presents evidence-ranked\n115:   candidates instead — GitHub's own record of the branch's PR and its\n116:   squash commit (queried through your existing `gh` session; cledger never\n117:   touches credentials), `(#N)` subject and squash-message corroboration,\n118:   and per-file content matches — each with its evidence spelled out, the\n119:   conversation-carrying commits named, and a ready-to-run\n120:   `cledger re-anchor <old-rev...> --onto REV` command that records the\n121:   mapping with you as the actor. Old SHAs are accepted even after the\n122:   commits themselves are garbage-collected. Forge lookups run only in this\n123:   explicit command (never the auto read path) and degrade to offline\n124:   evidence without `gh`; `--no-forge` or `{\"reanchor\": {\"forge\": false}}`\n125:   turns them off.\n126: - Opt out with `{\"reanchor\": {\"auto\": false}}`; the explicit command keeps\n127:   working either way.\n128: \n129: ## Adapters\n130: \n131: Supported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n132: \n133: Every captured event records the agent that served it — `producer.model`,\n134: `producer.provider`, `producer.source_version` — but only where the source\n135: states it; see the Roadmap entry below for what each adapter does and does\n136: not know.\n137: \n138: | Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n139: |---|---|---|---|\n140: | Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n141: | Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text but their embedded encrypted blocks are still dropped outright. |\n142: \n143: TODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n144: story looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n145: systems):\n146: \n147: - **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n148: - **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n149: - **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n150: - **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n151: - **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n152: - **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n153: - **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n154: - **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n155: - **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n156: \n157: ## Security & redaction\n158: \n159: Visible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n160: \n161: ### Redaction layers\n162: \n163: **Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n164: - Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n165: - Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n166: \n167: **Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n168: - Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n169: - Cost: manual configuration required.\n170: \n171: **Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n172: - Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n173: - Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n174: \n175: **Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n176: - Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n177: - Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n178: \n179: **Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n180: - Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n181: - Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n182: - Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n183: \n184: ### Commands\n185: \n186: `cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n187: \n188: `cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n189: \n190: ### Maximum safety recipe\n191: \n192: Keep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n193: \n194: ## Roadmap\n195: \n196: - **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — `loadConfig`\n197:   merges `~/.config/cledger/config.json` and `<repo>/.cledger.json` per\n198:   *top-level section*, not per key (`const redact = override.redact ??\n199:   base.redact`). So a repo that commits a `.cledger.json` with *any* `redact`\n200:   key — a `patterns` list, say — silently replaces the user's entire `redact`\n201:   section, and a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets: true` reverts to its\n202:   default of `false`. Capture-time scrubbing of learned secret values then\n203:   stops in that repo, with nothing printed and nothing in the ledger to show\n204:   it happened. Same shape as the pre-0.13.0 worktree bug: silent, fails open,\n205:   visible only if you go looking. Fix is a deep merge within each section, or\n206:   at minimum a warning when a repo config overrides a section the user config\n207:   had already set. Worth doing together with a general sweep for this class.\n208: \n209:   **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n210:   family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n211:   fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n212:   output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n213:   capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n214:   describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n215:   (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n216:   *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n217:   whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n218:   open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n219:   state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n220:   is itself captured.\n221: - **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n222:   structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n223:   and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n224:   gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n225:   the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n226:   *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n227:   `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n228:   Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n229:   made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n230:   findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n231:   its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n232:   instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n233:   understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n234:   workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n235:   open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n236:   than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n237:   printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n238:   the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n239:   terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n240:   simply going quiet for everyone.\n241: - **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n242:   (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n243:   and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n244:   abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n245:   collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n246:   storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n247: \n248:   Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n249:   unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n250:   push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n251:   the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n252:   the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n253:   recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n254: \n255:   *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n256:   reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n257:   `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n258:   conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n259:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n260:   back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n261:   behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n262:   to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n263: \n264:   *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n265:   implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n266:   append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n267:   whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n268:   your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n269:   silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n270:   current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n271:   show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n272:   from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n273:   adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n274: \n275:   *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n276:   carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n277:   shipping unrelated work.\n278: \n279:   *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n280:   git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n281:   another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n282:   and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n283:   skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n284:   falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n285:   the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n286:   place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n287:   marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n288: \n289:   *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n290:   no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n291:   one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n292:   the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n293:   Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n294:   were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n295:   selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n296:   entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n297: - **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n298:   the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n299:   reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n300:   knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n301:   the same mismatch. The default belongs in `readEvents` (with an explicit\n302:   opt-out) rather than in `export`, precisely because the consumers that\n303:   matter — intent-recall and anything else using the library — never go\n304:   through the CLI at all.\n305: - **Audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns** — this\n306:   repo's own dogfood allowlist (2026-07-22) picked up two `keyword-assignment`\n307:   fingerprints from this project's own development conversation: test code\n308:   like `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")`, where `\"github-token\"` is\n309:   a rule-id label, not a credential, but still trips \"a variable named\n310:   secret/token gets assigned something.\" Allowlisting is correct for now, but\n311:   it's a per-fingerprint, per-repo escape hatch — every downstream consumer of\n312:   cledger whose codebase talks *about* secrets (redaction tooling, security\n313:   tests, docs) will hit the same class of false positive independently, with\n314:   no shared fix. Periodically review what's accumulated in the allowlist: a\n315:   recurring pattern (e.g. \"the matched span is itself a rule id/label string,\n316:   not a real-looking token\") is a signal the `keyword-assignment` rule itself\n317:   should get smarter — not something every project should have to\n318:   allowlist for itself.\n319:   - **The false positive is self-perpetuating through documentation**\n320:     (observed 2026-07-27, from `intent-recall`): a design conversation that\n321:     quoted *this very roadmap entry* — the paragraph above, containing the\n322:     example `const secret = fakeSecret(\"github-token\")` — was itself flagged\n323:     with 6 `keyword-assignment` findings and held back from push. The\n324:     write-up of a false positive reproduces the false positive, in a\n325:     different repository, in a conversation transcript rather than in code.\n326:     Two things this exposes that the parent entry does not: (a) the allowlist\n327:     is per-repo, so a fingerprint retired here is re-encountered from scratch\n328:     by every downstream repo whose conversations read or discuss these docs —\n329:     and cledger's own documentation is now a reliable source of them; (b) the\n330:     flagged span was prose *about* a credential-shaped example and never a\n331:     credential, which is about as clean a signal as exists that the rule\n332:     wants a \"is this a discussion or an assignment?\" discriminator rather\n333:     than more allowlisting. Cheap partial fix worth weighing: treat a match\n334:     whose surrounding context is natural-language prose (a markdown\n335:     paragraph, a conversation turn) far more skeptically than one inside a\n336:     code block or a source file.\n337: - **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n338:   (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n339:   file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n340: - **`cledger redact`'s local purge leaves the value in the reflog** — the\n341:   squash is real but incomplete, and the command overstates what it achieved.\n342:   When the pre-redaction content was never pushed, `redactEvent` rebuilds the\n343:   notes ref as a parentless commit so the old chain becomes unreachable, and\n344:   reports `history squashed: yes`. Measured 2026-07-27: the pre-redaction blob\n345:   is still recoverable straight afterwards via\n346:   `git show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}')`,\n347:   because `update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded\n348:   commits. It stays recoverable for the reflog expiry window (90 days by\n349:   default) and only actually disappears after\n350:   `git reflog expire --expire=now` plus a pruning `git gc`. So today's\n351:   redaction is \"no reader will surface it\" rather than \"it is gone\", even in\n352:   the case the tool claims full success for.\n353:   *Fix, needs a decision before implementing:* expiring the ledger ref's\n354:   reflog is narrow and safe, but reclaiming the objects needs a prune, and\n355:   prune is repo-wide — it would also drop unreachable objects the user may\n356:   have wanted (dropped stashes, abandoned commits). Options are a scoped\n357:   `reflog expire` plus `git prune` accepted as repo-wide, or leaving the\n358:   objects and telling the truth in the output instead. What must not stay as\n359:   it is: reporting `history squashed: yes` when the value is still one\n360:   `git show` away.\n361:   *Unchanged either way:* once the ref has been pushed the squash is skipped\n362:   entirely and rotating the credential is the only real remedy — see below.\n363: - **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n364:   shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n365:   pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`. Three hard parts scoped so\n366:   far: (a) the `cat_sort_uniq` union merge means any collaborator still\n367:   holding the old blob silently re-introduces it on their next sync, so purge\n368:   must pair the force-push with a loud reset-don't-merge instruction and\n369:   detection of resurrected blobs on later fetches; (b) hosts retain\n370:   unreachable objects for a window and may serve them via API after a\n371:   force-push, so purge reduces exposure but rotating the leaked credential is\n372:   the only real remedy — output must say so plainly; (c) the companion\n373:   `redaction` event and the rewritten event's original id must survive the\n374:   purge (remove the secret's content, never the evidence a redaction\n375:   happened, and don't break rescan dedup).\n376: - **`re-anchor`** *(shipped, 0.8.0)* — events orphaned by squash merges or\n377:   history rewrites follow the surviving commit by default: append-only\n378:   `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the successor, deterministic ids\n379:   so concurrent detection dedups), read-time reachability resolving them\n380:   transitively, fetch-side detection via tree/patch-id exact matching, and\n381:   the `cledger re-anchor` command for dry runs and manual assertions. The\n382:   suggestion tier shipped in 0.9.0: unmatched branches get evidence-ranked\n383:   candidates (forge merge-commit assertion via `gh`, `(#N)`/squash-message\n384:   corroboration, per-file patch-id overlap), confirm-only, behind the forge\n385:   abstraction in `src/forge/` (GitHub driver first).\n386: - **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n387:   preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n388:   with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n389:   directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n390:   scope and capture triggers.\n391: - **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n392:   detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n393:   dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n394:   re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n395:   current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n396:   appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n397:   it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n398:   live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n399:   live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n400:   append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n401:   auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n402:   (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n403: - **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n404:   broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n405:   through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n406:   naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n407:   Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n408:   did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n409:   matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n410:   characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n411:   (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n412:   teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n413:   be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n414:   is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n415:   promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n416:   rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n417:   makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n418:   intended narrow form.\n419: - **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n420:   confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n421:   not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n422:   plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n423:   disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n424:   the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n425:   Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n426:   capture runs silently on every turn.\n427: - **Share local state across worktrees** *(shipped, 0.13.0)* — cledger kept\n428:   its local state under `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, which in a linked\n429:   worktree is that worktree's *private* directory\n430:   (`<main>/.git/worktrees/<name>/`), not the repo's shared one. Git is doing\n431:   exactly what it documents; the wrong assumption was that a repo has one git\n432:   dir. Everything cledger stores there is repo-wide, so a worktree behaved\n433:   like a separate repo, in four ways of descending severity: (a) the pre-push\n434:   hook was installed where **git never runs it** — git resolves hooks against\n435:   the common dir only — so a repo whose first capture happened in a worktree\n436:   got a hook that looked installed and silently never fired; (b) the\n437:   known-secrets store was invisible, failing **open**: values the user had\n438:   taught capture-time redaction to scrub were written to the ledger in the\n439:   clear, with nothing reporting it; (c) the allowlist was invisible, failing\n440:   closed, so already-dismissed scan findings blocked the ledger push again\n441:   (measured on this repo: 213 findings from a worktree vs 0 from the main\n442:   checkout); (d) capture cursors reset, re-scanning transcripts from the top.\n443:   Fixed by resolving a `commonDir` (`--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir`,\n444:   with a manual-resolve fallback for git < 2.31) and hanging all local state\n445:   and the hook path off it. Placement is irrelevant to the fix — git treats a\n446:   worktree nested under the repo and one at an arbitrary path identically, and\n447:   both are covered by tests. *Fallout that outlived the cause:* (d) also\n448:   **duplicated events**, because append-time dedup only consults the note of\n449:   the commit being written to, so a re-scan after HEAD moved filed a second\n450:   copy under a different anchor; once both anchors were reachable, reads\n451:   returned the turn twice (measured: 351 duplicated ids in this repo's own\n452:   ledger). The ledger is append-only, so those copies are permanent — reads\n453:   now collapse events by id, preferring the earliest `recorded_at` with the\n454:   canonical serialization as a deterministic tie-break. That path is not\n455:   worktree-specific; any forced rescan after HEAD moves can produce it.\n456:   *Deliberately unchanged:* `.cledger.json` stays a working-tree file, so it\n457:   is per-worktree unless committed — correct for versioned repo config, unlike\n458:   `.git`-side state. *Not migrated:* an allowlist or known-secrets store\n459:   written from a worktree before this fix stays orphaned in that worktree's\n460:   private dir; re-run `cledger allow` / `cledger redact --pattern` if you hit\n461:   it.\n462: - **Record which model/provider/CLI version served each turn** *(shipped,\n463:   0.12.0)* — `producer` previously said only which capture path recorded an\n464:   event (`source`