change their note lines, and under `cat_sort_uniq` the\n490:   rewritten and original lines both survive a merge with any peer still\n491:   holding the old one — two copies of one event. Old claude-code events lose\n492:   nothing permanent (their whole source line is in `raw.data`); old codex\n493:   events do, and re-capturing them does not help, since they dedup by id.\n494:   *Out of scope:* the rest of what those codex lines carry — `base_instructions`\n495:   (the full system prompt), sandbox/approval policy, reasoning effort — is\n496:   still not captured. That is a separate question (whole-session\n497:   configuration, not per-turn agent identity) with its own size and\n498:   redaction implications; this cut answers only \"what served this turn\".\n499: - **Preserve provider-encrypted reasoning as opaque state** *(shipped,\n500:   0.10.0)* — Codex's `reasoning` response_items carry `encrypted_content`\n501:   that only OpenAI's servers can decrypt; the client just round-trips the\n502:   blob, and replaying it restores the model's hidden reasoning\n503:   provider-side. Capture previously dropped these outright, which conflicted\n504:   with the record-everything-just-in-case posture: we discarded them because\n505:   *we* can't read them, yet the one party who can (the provider) already saw\n506:   the plaintext. Realization (2026-07-21, turnbridge): dropping them\n507:   permanently forecloses restoring reasoning when a Codex-origin\n508:   conversation is fabricated back into Codex on another machine (or after a\n509:   codex→claude→codex round trip). Empirically verified same-account and\n510:   cross-account (turnbridge `scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs`): a real\n511:   blob replayed in a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI\n512:   versions, and even a different paid ChatGPT account with identical token\n513:   accounting — the ciphertext is not keyed per-account. Shipped shape: a new\n514:   `reasoning` kind, provider-agnostic