{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"# Agent Note: Storage, Architecture, and Privacy\n\n## What It Stores\n\nAgent Note preserves four categories of data linked to commits:\n\n1. **Prompt/Response pairs** — The initial request to the AI and its answer\n2. **Changed files** — Which files the agent modified\n3. **AI Ratio** — \"A practical estimate of how much of the commit involved AI\"\n4. **Context notes** — Supplementary information when prompts alone lack sufficient meaning\n\n## Git Notes Architecture\n\nThe tool uses `refs/notes/agentnote` as its permanent storage layer. According to the article, \"The permanent record lives in `refs/notes/agentnote`.\" This approach keeps context attached to commits without modifying the commit history itself.\n\nSession data temporarily resides in `.git/agentnote/` before being consolidated into Git notes via hooks.\n\n## Transcript Handling\n\nAgent Note records **summaries rather than full transcripts**. The article indicates it captures the conversation elements needed for review—prompts and responses—but doesn't archive complete session transcripts. Support varies by agent; Claude Code provides the most detailed signals.\n\n## Linking Mechanism\n\n\"A Git hook links the session to the commit,\" then \"Agent Note writes a Git note for that commit.\" The process adds \"a short session trailer to the commit message\" while storing detailed records separately in notes.\n\n## Privacy and Opt-In Safeguards\n\nThe article emphasizes caution: \"Do not put secrets in prompts or responses.\" When notes are pushed, \"the team can read the saved conversation.\" However, no explicit opt-in mechanism or redaction tools are described in the content provided.","tool_use_id":"toolu_017mhUgdmfKUsbbDXp1R3jom","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintuna