{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"# How Git AI Works: Storage and Attribution\n\n## Storage Mechanism\n\nGit AI uses **git notes** as its primary storage mechanism, specifically under `refs/notes/ai`. As the documentation states, this approach offers \"Zero infrastructure — the notes live in your repo alongside the objects they describe.\"\n\n## What Gets Stored\n\nGit AI stores **attribution metadata only**, not full conversation transcripts. The notes contain:\n- File-to-session mappings with line ranges\n- JSON metadata per session including agent type, model used, and human author\n- A `messages_url` pointing to the full session context\n\nThe design explicitly keeps \"Sessions stay out of git\" — complete transcripts are stored separately (locally, in Git AI Cloud, or self-hosted), while commits only reference them.\n\n## Commit Linking\n\nAttribution attaches as git notes when you commit. Each note maps \"each file to the agent sessions that authored it and their line ranges,\" creating a permanent link between specific code lines and their generating sessions.\n\n## Push/Fetch Behavior\n\nAttribution syncs automatically: \"notes sync over `git push` and `git fetch`, so attribution reaches the remote the same way commits do.\" This occurs without requiring explicit opt-in per commit.\n\n## Redaction Support\n\nThe documentation does not mention redaction capabilities for Git AI's attribution system.","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VPxg7ZyE5rWafrrHKRdhrh","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:700cd82f-2f79-4cd6-b727-0f81230e0837#agent:af424cc63295b8016","parent":"claude-code:700cd82f-2f79-4cd6-b727-0f81230e0837","seq":38},"id":"ev1-2e4025a3e34450b39e77a59c33fabaecf185fffd09f0cd4df3a7a47c9f247efb","kind":"conver