{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"# Node.js Test Runner: Directory vs Glob vs File Arguments\n\nBased on the Node.js v26.5.0 documentation provided, here's how `node --test <path>` behaves with different argument types:\n\n## Default Behavior (No Arguments)\n\nWhen `node --test` is run **without** arguments, it automatically discovers test files matching these patterns:\n\n```\n**/*.test.{cjs,mjs,js}\n**/*-test.{cjs,mjs,js}\n**/*_test.{cjs,mjs,js}\n**/test-*.{cjs,mjs,js}\n**/test.{cjs,mjs,js}\n**/test/**/*.{cjs,mjs,js}\n```\n\n## With Explicit Path Arguments\n\nThe documentation states:\n\n> Alternatively, one or more glob patterns can be provided as the final argument(s) to the Node.js command, as shown below. Glob patterns follow the behavior of [`glob(7)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/glob.7.html). The glob patterns should be enclosed in double quotes on the command line to prevent shell expansion, which can reduce portability across systems.\n>\n> ```\n> node --test \"**/*.test.js\" \"**/*.spec.js\"\n> ```\n\n## Key Points\n\n1. **The documentation explicitly requires glob patterns**, not bare directory paths\n2. **No mention of automatic directory recursion** - you must use glob patterns like `\"**/*.test.js\"`\n3. **Direct file paths work**: You can specify individual test files\n4. **No recent behavior change is documented** in this version's release notes regarding directory argument handling\n\n**Conclusion**: Based on this documentation, `node --test` with a bare directory path is **not documented as supported**. You must use glob patterns (e.g., `node --test \"path/to/dir/**/*.test.js\"`) to recurse into directories, or specify individual files directly.","tool_use_id":"toolu_014VhJGD1c5cXYwy3Aih3MAN","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversa