iguration option that enables lifecycle hooks in Codex. According to the reference, it allows you to \"Enable lifecycle hooks loaded from `hooks.json` or inline `[hooks]`\" configuration.\n\n## Is it Required?\n\nNo, it is not required. The documentation indicates this feature is `off by default`, meaning you only need to enable it if you want to use hooks functionality.\n\n## Available Configuration Options\n\nThe reference documents several hook-related configuration settings:\n\n1. **`features.hooks`** (boolean) — Enables the hooks feature itself\n2. **`hooks`** (table) — Allows you to define lifecycle hooks directly in your `config.toml` file\n3. **`hooks.<Event>`** — Matcher groups for specific events like `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `SessionStart`, `Stop`, and others\n4. **`hooks.<Event>[].hooks`** — Handlers for matched events (currently supporting command hooks)\n5. **`hooks.<Event>[].hooks[].commandWindows`** — Windows-specific command overrides\n\nThe documentation notes that hooks use \"the same event schema as `hooks.json`\" and directs you to the Hooks guide for comprehensive examples and supported event types.","url":"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/config-file/config-reference"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"109627eb-dbed-427c-aeed-5fc0d0e24b98","version":"2.1.214"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T11:07:25.995Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"# git-memento: AI Coding Session Documentation Tool\n\n## Core Function\n\ngit-memento is a Git extension that preserves AI coding conversations by attaching transcripts to commits as git notes, creating an audit trail for AI-assisted development work.\n\n## Data Storage Architecture\n\n**Git Notes Integration:**\nThe tool leverages Git's native notes system to store session data. As documented, \"Notes are written with `git notes add -f -m '<markdown>' <commit-hash>`\". It maintains two separate note references:\n\n- `refs/notes/commits` - stores summary records (when summary mode is enabled)\n- `refs/notes/memento-full-audit` - preserves complete session transcripts\n\n**Multi-session Support:**\nThe system uses explicit delimiters for commits involving multiple AI providers:\n- `<!-- git-memento-sessions:v1 -->`\n- `<!-- git-memento-note-version:1 -->`\n\n## Conversation Linking\n\nEach commit can carry metadata blocks containing \"Provider:\" and \"Session ID:\" markers, directly linking the code change to specific AI conversations. Users invoke the tool via `git memento commit <session-id> -m \"message\"`, tying the session to that exact commit.\n\n## Provider Flexibility\n\nThe architecture supports multiple AI providers (Codex and Claude Code) through configurable environment variables and local git configuration, allowing teams to work with their preferred assistant.\n\n## Transparency vs. Privacy\n\nThe tool is **opt-in by design**—users explicitly choose which sessions to attach. No automatic redaction exists, but summary mode allows teams to generate condensed records instead of full transcripts, balancing transparency with information density.","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YEQ8EqCbV9LJdwXVVtiZ4f","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evint