{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"125\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n126\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n127\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n128\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n129\t\n130\t### Commands\n131\t\n132\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n133\t\n134\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n135\t\n136\t### Maximum safety recipe\n137\t\n138\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n139\t\n140\t## Roadmap\n141\t\n142\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n143\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n144\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n145\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n146\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n147\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`.\n148\t- **`re-anchor`** — reattach events orphaned by squash merges or history\n149\t  rewrites. The desired end state is that this happens by default: a squash\n150\t  commit should end up carrying the conversations of the branch it squashed.\n151\t  Hard part: squashes often happen off-machine (GitHub \"Squash and merge\"),\n152\t  so detection needs patch-id/branch-tip matching after fetch, not a local\n153\t  hook.\n154\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n155\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n156\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n157\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n158\t  scope and capture triggers.\n159\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n160\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n161\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n162\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning adapter's\n163\t  current convert path and, for the ones this version can now interpret,\n164\t  appends the proper `conversation_turn` plus a `supersession` event linking\n165\t  it to the raw placeholder. The turn is reconstructed with the exact id a\n166\t  live capture would produce (same seq/session/timestamp handling), so a later\n167\t  live capture of the same session dedups rather than duplicating; the run is\n168\t  append-only and idempotent. *Remaining:* this is a manual command —\n169\t  auto-running it after an adapter/version bump on capture is deferred\n170\t  (detecting \"the adapter changed\" is its own problem).\n171\t- **Capture/scan redaction feedback loop** *(shipped, 0.6.1 + 0.7.0)* — the\n172\t  broad pre-push scan can flag a value the conservative capture tier let\n173\t  through, and because the sessions spent fixing it are themselves captured, a\n174\t  naive fix-sync-fix cycle can re-ingest the same raw value indefinitely.\n175\t  Allowlisting escapes it (fingerprint-keyed), but per-event redaction alone\n176\t  did not. Two channels are now closed: (a) the sync-report output masks the\n177\t  matched span entirely — surrounding context plus fingerprint, zero secret\n178\t  characters — so a captured report can't re-seed the finding it describes\n179\t  (0.6.1); (b) the opt-in `knownSecrets` store lets `cledger redact --pattern`\n180\t  teach capture-time redaction the exact confirmed values, so they can never\n181\t  be re-captured raw (0.7.0). *Deferred / rejected:* the fuller \"capture-tier\n182\t  is a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and set aside —\n183\t  promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently\n184\t  rewrite innocent text, violating the near-zero-false-positive invariant that\n185\t  makes capture-time rewriting safe. The value-based learning above is the\n186\t  intended narrow form.\n187\t- **Encrypt the known-secrets store at rest** — the opt-in store aggregates\n188\t  confirmed secret plaintext into one predictably-named local file. This does\n189\t  not change the transport threat model (the same values already sit in\n190\t  plaintext transcripts), but the *aggregation* is a distinct accidental-read /\n191\t  disk-snoop / backup-scoop risk. Owner-only `0600` perms shipped in 0.7.1;\n192\t  the real remaining work is encryption with an OS-keychain-held key (macOS\n193\t  Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI), which must decrypt *non-interactively* since\n194\t  capture runs silently on every turn.\n195\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n196\