{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Conversation Ledger\n2\t\n3\tDurable, attributable records of visible coding-agent conversations, stored in\n4\tyour repository as git notes.\n5\t\n6\t`cledger` (also installed as `conversation-ledger`) is a small standalone\n7\tutility in the unix tradition: it appends immutable JSONL evidence events to\n8\t`refs/notes/conversation-ledger`, anchored to the commit you were on when the\n9\tconversation happened. Records ride the commit DAG — merge a branch and its\n10\tconversations come along — never touch your working tree or `git status`, and\n11\tonly leave your machine when you explicitly `cledger sync`.\n12\t\n13\tConversation Ledger is the neutral foundation of a small ecosystem:\n14\t\n15\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n16\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n17\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n18\t\n19\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n20\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n21\t\n22\t## Install\n23\t\n24\t```sh\n25\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n26\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n27\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n31\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n32\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n33\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n34\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n35\tduplicates.\n36\t\n37\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n38\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n39\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n40\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n41\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n42\t\n43\t## Use\n44\t\n45\t```sh\n46\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n47\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n48\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n49\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n50\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n51\tcledger sync                    # explicit opt-in fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n52\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\t## Adapters\n56\t\n57\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n58\t\n59\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n60\t|---|---|---|---|\n61\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n62\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. |\n63\t\n64\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n65\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n66\tsystems):\n67\t\n68\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n69\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n70\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n71\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n72\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n73\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n74\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n75\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n76\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n77\t\n78\t## Roadmap\n79\t\n80\t- **Secret redaction at capture** — detect/scrub API keys and credentials in\n81\t  tool output before events are written; the highest-priority open item.\n82\t- **`re-anchor`** — reattach events orphaned by squash merges or history\n83\t  rewrites. The desired end state is that this happens by default: a squash\n84\t  commit should end up carrying the conversations of the branch it squashed.\n85\t  Hard part: squashes often happen off-machine (GitHub \"Squash and merge\"),\n86\t  so detection needs patch-id/branch-tip matching after fetch, not a local\n87\t  hook.\n88\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n89\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n90\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n91\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n92\t  scope and capture triggers.\n93\t- **Format-drift resilience** — adapters currently skip transcript line\n94\t  types they don't recognize, so a harness update that introduces new\n95\t  message types would silently leave that content uncaptured (see\n96\t  \"Versioning\" in the design doc). At minimum: count and warn about\n97\t  skipped-but-substantive lines; possibly preserve them raw-only.\n98\t- **Purge tooling** — true content removal behind a `redaction` event.\n99\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n100\t\n101\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n102\t\n103\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n104\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n105\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n106\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n107\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n108\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n109\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n110\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n111\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n112\t\n113\t## License\n114\t\n115\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n116\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01R6xAAfDyHH7MmfN9rAjvwd","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707#agent:acceac9e5b51043d7","parent":"claude-code:353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707","seq":6},"id":"ev1-7ad5e618c00fc734bf3abcae908b07a59bef5d3de8b4c5de0362f6f392438e54","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-20T03:10:55.997Z","producer":{"session_id":"353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.215","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"da