cosystem:\n15\t\n16\t- `conversation-ledger` stores normalized, provenance-preserving conversation evidence.\n17\t- `turnbridge` records and transfers visible conversations between coding CLIs.\n18\t- `intent-recall` later retrieves evidence to help agents reason about original and superseded intent.\n19\t\n20\tThe ledger itself never summarizes, ranks, or interprets. It stores what was\n21\tvisibly said, by whom, against which commit — nothing more.\n22\t\n23\t## Install\n24\t\n25\t```sh\n26\tgit clone https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger\n27\tcd conversation-ledger && npm install && npm link   # npm publish coming later\n28\tcledger install all    # hook capture into Claude Code + Codex CLI\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\t`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`\n32\tand a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n33\tbacking both up first. From then on, every completed turn in any git\n34\trepository is captured automatically, in the same universal format. Capture is\n35\tidempotent — events are content-derived, so re-scanning a transcript never\n36\tduplicates.\n37\t\n38\tTwo one-time activation notes: Claude Code reads hooks at session start, so\n39\tcapture begins with your next session; Codex requires you to trust new hooks\n40\tinteractively — open `codex`, run `/hooks`, and approve the `cledger hook\n41\tcodex` command once. Missed turns are never lost either way: `cledger capture\n42\t<source> --transcript PATH` backfills any native transcript, idempotently.\n43\t\n44\t## Use\n45\t\n46\t```sh\n47\tcledger log                     # events on the current branch (via commit reachability)\n48\tcledger log --all --json        # every event, as JSONL for jq & friends\n49\tcledger conversations           # sessions touching this branch\n50\tcledger show claude-code:3f9a   # replay one conversation in order\n51\tcledger export > ledger.jsonl   # lossless dump, incl. source-native payloads\n52\tcledger sync                    # explicit fetch/merge/push of the ledger ref\n53\techo '{\"kind\":\"decision\",...}' | cledger append   # any tool can write events\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t## Sharing (transport)\n57\t\n58\tThe ledger travels with normal git use, no extra commands:\n59\t\n60\t- **Push**: the first capture in a repo installs a `pre-push` hook (chaining\n61\t  safely onto any existing shell hook; backing off with a warning when\n62\t  `core.hooksPath`/husky owns the hooks). On `git push` it pushes the ledger\n63\t  ref alongside, gated by the secret scan. A finding holds back **only the\n64\t  ledger** — your code push proceeds — unless you opt into\n65\t  `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`, which aborts the whole push.\n66\t- **Fetch**: the same first capture adds a fetch refspec on `origin`, so\n67\t  `git fetch`/`git pull` stages teammates' events; any read command\n68\t  (`cledger log`, `show`, ...) folds them in via the conflict-free\n69\t  `cat_sort_uniq` union. The local ref is never force-overwritten.\n70\t- **Opting out**: `{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}` in\n71\t  `.cledger.json` or `~/.config/cledger/config.json`, or just delete the\n72\t  marked block from `.git/hooks/pre-push`. `cledger sync` always works\n73\t  explicitly either way.\n74\t\n75\tIf git has no author identity configured (`user.email` unset), human turns\n76\tare recorded unattributed; `cledger install` warns about this. cledger uses\n77\tthe same identity a commit would be authored under (config or environment)\n78\tand never guesses a hostname-based one.\n79\t\n80\t## Adapters\n81\t\n82\tSupported today (built-in, hook-based, per-turn):\n83\t\n84\t| Source | Trigger | Transcript store | Notes |\n85\t|---|---|---|---|\n86\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n87\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured with their visible text; their encrypted blocks are never stored, same policy as reasoning. |\n88\t\n89\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n90\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n91\tsystems):\n92\t\n93\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n94\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n95\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n96\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n97\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n98\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n99\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n100\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n101\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n102\t\n103\t## Security & redaction\n104\t\n105\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n106\t\n107\t### Redaction layers\n108\t\n109\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n110\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n111\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n112\t\n113\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n114\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n115\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n116\t\n117\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n118\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n119\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n120\t\n121\t**Known-secret learning** (opt-in, default off): Remember the exact values a `cledger redact <event-id> --pattern` scrubbed, then exact-match them out of every future capture (under a `known-secret` rule id). Enable with `{\"redact\": {\"knownSecrets\": true}}`. Confirmed values are stored locally under `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json`, written owner-only (`0600`) — never tracked or pushed by git, the same tier as the allowlist. Only `--pattern` feeds it; values under 8 chars are never remembered.\n122\t- Threat addressed: the capture/scan feedback loop — a value the broad sync scan catches but the conservative capture tier misses gets re-ingested raw every time you revisit it while fixing it. Once redacted, capture learns it and it can never be re-captured raw again.\n123\t- Cost of enabling: confirmed secret plaintext lives in a new (local, unshared) file; like env masking, capture becomes machine-dependent for those values. Concretely, re-capturing a source line whose value is now remembered yields *scrubbed* content and therefore a different event id, so it no longer dedups against the pre-existing event — you get a second, also-scrubbed copy rather than a resurrected secret. Id churn, not leakage.\n124\t\n125\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings abort the ledger push with a report and remediation instructions; the report shows ~20 chars of surrounding context with the matched value **fully masked** (`<redacted>`, zero secret characters) so the report itself can't re-seed the next scan. In the pre-push hook, a finding holds back only the ledger and lets your code push proceed unless `{\"transport\": {\"strict\": true}}`.\n126\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n127\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n128\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n129\t\n130\t### Commands\n131\t\n132\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n133\t\n134\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n135\t\n136\t### Maximum safety recipe\n137\t\n138\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n139\t\n140\t## Roadmap\n141\t\n142\t- **Path-based capture exclusion** — the path half of the redaction config\n143\t  (\"never record reads of `secrets/**`\"); requires correlating `tool_use`\n144\t  file paths with their `tool_result` events.\n145\t- **Post-push purge** — true content removal after the ledger ref has been\n146\t  shared (force-push + collaborator re-fetch coordination); the local\n147\t  pre-push squash shipped with `cledger redact`.\n148\t- **`re-anchor`** — reattach events orphaned by squash merges or history\n149\t  rewrites. The desired end state is that this happens by default: a squash\n150\t  commit should end up carrying the conversations of the branch it squashed.\n151\t  Hard part: squashes often happen off-machine (GitHub \"Squash and merge\"),\n152\t  so detection needs patch-id/branch-tip matching after fetch, not a local\n153\t  hook.\n154\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n155\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n156\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n157\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n158\t  scope and capture triggers.\n159\t- **Format-drift: re-normalization** — unrecognized transcript line types are\n160\t  detected (0.4.0), preserved raw-only as `unrecognized` events rather than\n161\t  dropped (0.5.0), and now re-normalizable (0.6.0): `cledger renormalize`\n162\t  re-feeds each preserved line's `raw.data` through its owning 