pear in `git status`; sharing is a\n77\t   `cledger sync` (fetch → `git notes merge -s cat_sort_uniq` → push) of\n78\t   that single ref — explicit-only today, with an optional on-by-default\n79\t   pre-push hook planned so it rides normal `git push` (still\n80\t   user-disableable; the E scan gates it either way).\n81\t\n82\tEvents captured before the first commit exists (unborn `HEAD`) queue in\n83\t`.git/conversation-ledger/pending.jsonl` and flush into the first real\n84\tanchor. Everything under `.git/conversation-ledger/` (pending queue, adapter\n85\tcursors, lock) is local, rebuildable state — never the record of truth.\n86\t\n87\t### Known limitation: squash merges\n88\t\n89\tA squash merge discards the source branch's commits, so their conversations\n90\tremain in the ledger (notes are enumerable regardless of reachability;\n91\t`cledger log --all` sees them) but drop out of the target branch's\n92\treachability view. A future `re-anchor` operation is the intended fix.\n93\t\n94\t## Capture adapters\n95\t\n96\t`cledger install` registers per-turn hooks globally: Claude Code\n97\t(`Stop`/`SessionEnd` in `~/.claude/settings.json`) and Codex CLI\n98\t(`[[hooks.Stop]]` in `~/.codex/config.toml`). Hooks receive\n99\t`session_id`/`transcript_path`/`cwd` on stdin, no-op silently outside git\n100\trepositories, never fail the user's session, and re-scan tolerantly —\n101\tidempotent ids make duplicate capture harmless. Per-session cursors under\n102\t`.git/conversation-ledger/cursors/` are a pure optimization.\n103\t\n104\t## Privacy and integrity\n105\t\n106\tVisible tool output can contain secrets. The trust boundary is transport:\n107\tnative transcripts already sit in plaintext on the capturing machine, so\n108\tredaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. But once a\n109\tsecret reaches a note, removal is expensive (the notes ref's own history\n110\tretains prior blobs), so prevention layers run at capture and the last\n111\tcheckpoint runs at sync. Content hashes (event ids) make accidental\n112\tmutation detectable.\n113\t\n114\t### Redaction layers\n115\t\n116\tDefense in depth, ordered by where they run and what they may do:\n117\t\n118\t- **A. Capture-time pattern redaction (default on).** A deliberately\n119\t  conservative, versioned ruleset of unambiguous secret formats — prefixed\n120\t  API tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`,\n121\t  `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private-key blocks, JWTs — applied to every\n122\t  draft inside `appendEvents`, before ids are computed. Matches in both\n123\t  `content` and `raw.data` are replaced with a deterministic placeholder\n124\t  (see below). No entropy heuristics here: a false positive silently\n125\t  rewrites the record, which violates the preservation commitment, so\n126\t  capture-tier rules must be near-zero-false-positive by construction.\n127\t- **B. External scanners as test oracle only.** gitleaks/trufflehog-class\n128\t  rulesets are used in the test suite to prove the capture tier catches\n129\t  what it claims (secret corpus in, zero findings out) — never vendored\n130\t  into the runtime path.\n131\t- **C. Env-value masking (opt-in, default off).** Scrub exact values of\n132\t  local environment variables / `.env` entries from captured content.\n133\t  Highest recall for unstructured secrets, but `.env` is often plain\n134\t  config, and the transform depends on machine state — so it is\n135\t  nondeterministic across rescans (id churn is the documented cost of\n136\t  opting in).\n137\t- **D. User pattern rules (config).** Extra redaction regexes merged into\n138\t  the capture tier from config. Path-based exclusion (\"never record reads\n139\t  of `secrets/**`\") is planned; it requires correlating `tool_use` inputs\n140\t  with `tool_result` events and is not in v1.\n141\t- **E. Sync-time scan (default on, tiered).** Before push, scan only the\n142\t  events the remote does not yet have. The default profile runs\n143\t  medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run for events captured\n144\t  under older rules, keyword-anchored assignments, URL credentials); on a\n145\t  hit the push blocks with an interactive finding report — allowlist the\n146\t  false positive (persisted by fingerprint) or `cledger redact` the real\n147\t  secret while purge is still a local operation. Entropy heuristics live\n148\t  behind an opt-in paranoid tier; `--no-scan`/config disables the gate\n149\t  entirely. Scan-tier rules may be noisy precisely because they only warn\n150\t  a human — they never rewrite anything.\n151\t\n152\t### Redaction metadata\n153\t\n154\tA capture-time redaction replaces the matched span with\n155\t`[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]` — fingerprint is a truncated\n156\t`sha256(secret)`, letting identical secrets correlate across events without\n157\tbeing recoverable (brute-forceable only for low-entropy values, which the\n158\tcapture tier does not target). The event also carries a `redactions` array\n159\t(rule id, ruleset version, fingerprint, location path); it sits outside the\n160\tidentity subset, since the rewritten `content` already determines the id.\n161\tDeterminism rule: capture-tier redaction must be a pure function of source\n162\tcontent + versioned ruleset, or rescans duplicate events. Ruleset upgrades\n163\ttherefore change ids on forced rescan — same accepted caveat as identity\n164\tstamping; cursors prevent it in normal operation.\n165\t\n166\tA human redaction of an *existing* event (the E flow) instead rewrites the\n167\tnote line and appends a companion `redaction` event with `links.redacts`,\n168\tthen squashes the local notes ref so the prior blob is unreachable —\n169\thonest history without secret retention. Post-push purge (force-push +\n170\tcollaborator coordination) is deliberately separate, deferred tooling.\n171\t\n172\t## Versioning against harness format changes\n173\t\n174\tThree layers exist today:\n175\t\n176\t1. `schema` on