{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"60\t|---|---|---|---|\n61\t| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. |\n62\t| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. |\n63\t\n64\tTODO adapters, roughly in order of how ledger-friendly their storage/hook\n65\tstory looks (all have local session stores; most grew Claude-Code-style hook\n66\tsystems):\n67\t\n68\t- **Gemini CLI** — JSON chats under `~/.gemini/tmp/<hash>/chats/`; documented hooks.\n69\t- **Kimi CLI** — `~/.kimi-code/` sessions; Claude-Code-inspired lifecycle hooks.\n70\t- **GitHub Copilot CLI** — `~/.copilot/session-state/`; documented hooks dirs.\n71\t- **Factory droid** — `~/.factory/` sessions; `hooks.json`.\n72\t- **Qwen Code** — `~/.qwen/projects/*/chats/`; hooks system.\n73\t- **opencode** — SQLite under `~/.local/share/opencode/`; plugin-API events rather than shell hooks.\n74\t- **Cursor (`cursor-agent` CLI)** — `~/.cursor/chats`; hooks exist, but IDE-side chats live in editor-internal storage.\n75\t- **aider** — plain `.aider.chat.history.md`; no hook mechanism found, would need file watching.\n76\t- **Goose / Amp** — SQLite store / cloud-synced threads; hook stories unclear or absent.\n77\t\n78\t## Security & redaction\n79\t\n80\tVisible tool output can contain secrets: API keys in error messages, credentials in logs, etc. The trust boundary is transport — native transcripts are plaintext locally, so redaction protects the *shared* record, not the local disk. Once content reaches a note, removal is expensive, so prevention runs first at capture, with the last checkpoint at sync.\n81\t\n82\t### Redaction layers\n83\t\n84\t**Capture-time redaction** (default on): Replaces prefixed tokens (`ghp_…`, `sk-ant-…`, `AKIA…`, `xox…-`, `AIza…`, `glpat-…`, `npm_…`, `sk_live_…`), PEM private keys, JWTs with `[REDACTED:<rule-id>:<fingerprint>]`. Conservative, near-zero false-positive ruleset applied before event ids are computed.\n85\t- Threat addressed: API tokens and private keys in tool output land in shared history.\n86\t- Cost of disabling: `.cledger.json` `{\"redact\": {\"capture\": false}}` — every secret lands verbatim.\n87\t\n88\t**Custom patterns**: Extend capture rules via `{\"redact\": {\"patterns\": [{\"id\": \"my-rule\", \"pattern\": \"...\"}]}}` in `~/.config/cledger/config.json` (global) or `<repo>/.cledger.json` (repo wins).\n89\t- Threat addressed: domain-specific secrets unmatched by standard rules.\n90\t- Cost: manual configuration required.\n91\t\n92\t**Env-value masking** (opt-in, default off): Scrub exact values of environment variables and `.env` entries via `{\"redact\": {\"env\": true}}`.\n93\t- Threat addressed: unstructured secrets in local config.\n94\t- Cost of enabling: `.env` plain-config values get masked too; machine-dependent (rescans produce duplicate events with different ids).\n95\t\n96\t**Sync-time scan** (default on, tiered): Before any push, scans only new events with medium/high-precision rules (capture ruleset re-run, keyword assignments like `password=`, URL credentials). Findings block the push interactively.\n97\t- Threat addressed: secrets from older capture rules or new tool formats slipping through.\n98\t- Cost of disabling: `{\"scan\": {\"tier\": \"off\"}}` — secrets in tool output push silently.\n99\t- Remediation paths: `cledger redact <event-id>` (real secrets), `cledger allow <fingerprint>` (false positives), `cledger sync --no-scan` (bypass once). `--paranoid` tier adds entropy-based detection (noisier but broader).\n100\t\n101\t### Commands\n102\t\n103\t`cledger redact <event-id> (--pattern REGEX | --all) [--reason TEXT]`: Rewrites stored event to placeholder + audit metadata, appends a `redaction` event, and squashes local notes history so prior content is unrecoverable (pre-push only; post-push purge tooling is planned, not yet built).\n104\t\n105\t`cledger scan [--paranoid]`: Standalone check with exit 1 on findings — CI-friendly.\n106\t\n107\t### Maximum safety recipe\n108\t\n109\tKeep all defaults (capture and sync scan on), add repo-specific patterns in `.cledger.json` for domain-specific secrets, enable env masking only if `.env` holds secrets (not config), run `cledger scan` in CI, and treat `--no-scan` as a deliberate exception you document.\n110\t\n111\t## Roadmap\n112\t\n113\t- **Secret redaction at capture** — detect/scrub API keys and credentials in\n114\t  tool output before events are written; the highest-priority open item.\n115\t- **`re-anchor`** — reattach events orphaned by squash merges or history\n116\t  rewrites. The desired end state is that this happens by default: a squash\n117\t  commit should end up carrying the conversations of the branch it squashed.\n118\t  Hard part: squashes often happen off-machine (GitHub \"Squash and merge\"),\n119\t  so detection needs patch-id/branch-tip matching after fetch, not a local\n120\t  hook.\n121\t- **Harness-artifact capture** — decide whether the ledger should also\n122\t  preserve valuable non-git-controlled agent artifacts that normally die\n123\t  with a worktree or live outside the repo (e.g. Claude Code auto-memory\n124\t  directories, session state). They fit the `document` kind; the question is\n125\t  scope and capture triggers.\n126\t- **Format-drift resilience** — adapters currently skip transcript line\n127\t  types they don't recognize, so a harness update that introduces new\n128\t  message types would silently leave that content uncaptured (see\n129\t  \"Versioning\" in the design doc). At minimum: count and warn about\n130\t  skipped-but-substantive lines; possibly preserve them raw-only.\n131\t- **Purge tooling** — true content removal behind a `redaction` event.\n132\t- **Sub-turn citation anchors** for downstream consumers like intent-recall.\n133\t\n134\t## Storage model, in one paragraph\n135\t\n136\tOne git note per anchor commit under `refs/notes/conversation-ledger`; the\n137\tnote body is one canonical-JSON event per line, sorted and unique, with all\n138\tcontent inline (GC-safe, no out-of-repo pointers). Concurrent branches merge\n139\tconflict-free via git's `cat_sort_uniq` notes strategy. Events record\n140\trepository, branch, HEAD, dirty-tree fingerprint, actor, timestamps, and\n141\tcapture mechanism. Redaction appends a `redaction` event rather than silently\n142\trewriting history. See [docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md](docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md)\n143\tfor details and [docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md](docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md) for what this\n144\ttool deliberately refuses to become.\n145\t\n146\t## License\n147\t\n148\t[MIT](LICENSE)\n149\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9VKQnbGjcjbjxvGMfxLVn","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"de79e501289e2e19946780867779ef465ead2124","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707#agent:acceac9e5b51043d7","parent":"claude-code:353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707","seq":15},"id":"ev1-bcdf1cc3e608a1efdee8ceca18727dee928af7601f6b77fc5184258cb11ff2c2","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-20T03:12:26.217Z","producer":{"session_id":"353eecbb-eaf7-4854-a575-a9d56e290707","source":"claude-c