aths are used, the available-skills catalog also provides a mapping from aliases such as `r0` to their filesystem roots. Expand the alias before accessing the skill.\\n- Trigger rules: If the user names an available skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches an available skill's description, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill is not available or its `SKILL.md` cannot be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\\n- How to use a skill:\\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, the main agent must read its `SKILL.md` completely before taking task actions. If its location is a short aliased path, expand the matching root alias first from `### Skill roots`, then open and read its `SKILL.md` completely before taking task actions. For a filesystem path, open the file. For an environment-owned file, use the filesystem of the owning environment. For an orchestrator reference, call `skills.list` with `{\\\"authority\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"orchestrator\\\"}}`, select the matching package, and pass its `main_resource` to `skills.read`. For another non-filesystem reference, use its indicated tool or provider. If a read is truncated or paginated, continue until EOF.\\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references another file or resource, use the same access mechanism. Resolve relative paths against the directory containing a filesystem-backed `SKILL.md`. For orchestrator skills, pass the exact referenced resource identifier with the same authority and package to `skills.read`; do not treat `skill://` identifiers as filesystem paths.\\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, use its routing instructions to identify what is required for the task. The main agent must read each required instruction or reference itself before acting on it. Do not delegate reading, summarizing, or interpreting skill instructions to a subagent. Subagents may still perform task work when the selected skill allows it.\\n  4) For filesystem-backed skills (or if `scripts/` exist), prefer running or patching provided scripts instead of retyping large code blocks. For orchestrator skills, use `skills.read` and the available tools; do not invent a local path.\\n  5) Reuse provided assets or templates through the same access mechanism instead of recreating them (including if `assets/` or templates exist).\\n- Coordination and sequencing:\\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\\n  - Announce which skills you're using and why. If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\\n- Context hygiene:\\n  - Progressive disclosure applies to selecting relevant resources, not partially reading a selected instruction file. Do not load unrelated references, scripts, or assets.\\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer files or resources directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless blocked.\\n  - When variants exist, select only the relevant references and note the choice.\\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill cannot be applied cleanly, state the issue, choose the best alternative, and continue.\\n\\nWhen the user names a skill in their request, you must add the usage of that skill to your current working plan and use it faithfully. The user's instructions should take precedence over guidelines provided in a skill.\\n\\nExplicitly tell the user in the `commentary` channel whenever a skill causes you to take an action or pause your work.\\n\\nWhen using a skill the user did not explicitly name, follow this procedure:\\n\\n- First, tell the user in the commentary channel **why** you are using the skill.\\n- Then, use the skill as long as it stays within the scope of the task.\\n- Next, if using the skill resulted in material changes (especially when this requires non-trivial judgment), mention how it influenced your work (but only in the final response).\\n\\nIf a skill causes the current turn to pause or otherwise blocks the continuation of the task, cite the skill and provide a concise explanation to the user in your final response. Do not cite skills you merely inspected.\\n\"\n    },\n    \"history_mode\": \"legacy\",\n    \"context_window\": {\n      \"window_id\": \"019f7624-d816-72b1-b8da-fc81898f5be1\"\n    }\n  }\n}"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"4bc45c4f-63e5-41ec-98df-d5229d255d94","version":"2.1.214"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T11:07:26.954Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"=== a user message with tool_result content ===\n{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"see ~/TODO.md. what's incompleted?\"}\n{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_011e8nRtXGKyoTJQYasAQtLc\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t# Mac Setup — Carried-Over TODOs\\n2\\t\\n3\\tPicked up from a Mac setup session on 2026-07-15/16. Context notes included so a fresh Claude Code session can pick these up without re-deriving everything.\\n4\\t\\n5\\t## 1. iCloud Documents & Desktop cleanup\\n6\\t\\n7\\t- [ ] General organization pass on iCloud Documents/Desktop (described as \\\"nightmarish\\\")\\n8\\t- [ ] **Security issue to resolve:** there's a folder on the iCloud Desktop called `password screenshots` — screenshots of passwords stored as plain image files. Decide: migrate those credentials into the Apple Passwords app (already installed, macOS default) or a dedicated password manager, then securely delete the screenshot folder once migrated.\\n9\\t\\n10\\t## 2. LaTeX distribution\\n11\\t\\n12\\t- [x] Decide on and install a LaTeX setup — wanted \\\"something\\\" but hadn't settled on which distribution.\\n13\\t  - Went with BasicTeX + tlmgr (latexmk, collection-fontsrecommended/latexextra/bibtexextra/binextra). LaTeX Workshop installed in VSCodium (Open VSX), configured for manual build (no autocompile) with absolute binary paths + explicit PATH env to work around macOS's stale-session-PATH issue after a fresh install. Verified end-to-end on a real Overleaf-synced paper (fonts, math, bibtex, custom .sty).\\n14\\t- Worth considering: MiKTeX (was on the old MacBook Air) vs. TeX Live / BasicTeX (the standard on Linux, so closer to cluster parity — MiKTeX is more historically Windows-native even though it's cross-platform now). BasicTeX + `tlmgr` gives a small footprint with on-demand package installs if the full TeX Live install feels heavy.\\n15\\t\\n16\\t## 3. SSH jump host: personal Mac → work laptop → work cluster, usable from VSCodium\\n17\\t\\n18\\t- **Why now:** company is rolling out a policy where personal laptops will only be allowed to SSH into the *work laptop*, not directly into the cluster — the work laptop will still be allowed to SSH into the cluster. So the personal Mac needs to hop through the work laptop to reach the cluster.\\n19\\t- [ ] Add a `ProxyJump` entry to `~/.ssh/config` — something like:\\n20\\t  ```\\n21\\t  Host work-laptop\\n22\\t      HostName <work-laptop-address>\\n23\\t      User <username>\\n24\\t      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work-laptop\\n25\\t\\n26\\t  Host work-cluster\\n27\\t      HostName <cluster-address>\\n28\\t      User <username>\\n29\\t      ProxyJump work-laptop\\n30\\t      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work-cluster\\n31\\t  ```\\n32\\t- [ ] Register the `id_ed25519_work-laptop.pub` key with the work laptop's `authorized_keys`, and `id_ed25519_work-cluster.pub` with the cluster's — both keys already exist on this Mac (generated during initial setup) but were never registered anywhere.\\n33\\t- ⚠️ **Known blocker to resolve:** VS Code's Remote-SSH extension (`ms-vscode-remote.remote-ssh`) is Microsoft-proprietary and isn't distributed through Open VSX — the marketplace VSCodium uses by default. Since this setup deliberately chose VSCodium over regular VS Code, Remote-SSH may not install normally. Options to evaluate next session: the community `open-remote-ssh` fork (Open VSX-compatible), switching back to regular VS Code just for this workflow, or another remote-dev extension that isn't MS-gated.\\n34\\t\\n35\\t## 4. Reconnect reMarkable tablet — DONE 2026-07-16\\n36\\t\\n37\\t- [x] reMarkable app + reManager are both installed, tablet paired and reManager confirmed syncing.\\n38\\t- [x] Mac ⇄ tablet SSH over local WiFi (dedicated key `~/.ssh/id_ed25519_remarkable`, `ssh remarkable` alias in `~/.ssh/config`).\\n39\\t- [x] Switched the tablet's outbound SSH target from the old (sold) MacBook Air to this Mac. Root cause chain, for future reference:\\n40\\t  - The tablet's reMarkable-OS kernel has no TUN device, so its Tailscale runs userspace-only — plain `ssh 100.x` never routes anywhere. The prior setup already solved this correctly with `/home/root/bin/tssh`, which proxies through `tailscale nc` as an OpenSSH `ProxyCommand`, using the real OpenSSH client at `/opt/bin/ssh` (not the tablet's built-in Dropbear `ssh`, which can't parse modern `openssh-key-v1`-format keys at all — that's a real, confirmed Dropbear limitation, not a config issue).\\n41\\t  - The only actually stale thing was `/home/root/bin/mac`, a one-line shortcut hardcoded to the old MacBook Air's Tailscale IP (`100.72.225.106`). Updated it to this Mac's Tailscale IP (`100.105.108.44`). Verified end-to-end: `mac \\\"echo ok\\\"` from the tablet works.\\n42\\t  - Remote Login (SSH server) enabled on this Mac (was off by default on the fresh install).\\n43\\t  - Dead end explored and reverted: temporarily restricted this Mac's sshd `KexAlgorithms` to non-quantum-safe algorithms chasing a red herring — reverted, back to full defaults.\\n44\\t\\n45\\t## 5. Expose ds4 as a semi-public API for the reMarkable (and friends), only while the laptop is plugged in\\n46\\t\\n47\\t- **Goal:** let the reMarkable tablet — and some external people (\\\"friends\\\") — hit the local `ds4-server` (DeepSeek V4 Flash, already downloaded/working) over the network, but not have it running unconditionally or fully open to the world.\\n48\\t- [ ] Decide exposure mechanism — Tailscale is already installed, two real options:\\n49\\t  - **Tailscale Serve** — private to devices on the tailnet only; \\\"friends\\\" would need to be invited onto the tailnet (simplest, most secure, but not truly public)\\n50\\t  - **Tailscale Funnel** — actually publicly reachable over HTTPS from the open internet, no tailnet invite needed for friends, but wider exposure surface\\n51\\t- [ ] Decide access control — \\\"friends\\\" implies more 