{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"Web search results for query: \"\"AI conversation\" provenance git commit trailer prompt provenance github\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Assisted-by git trailer: provenance for AI-assisted commits\",\"url\":\"https://www.baristalabs.io/blog/ai-assisted-commits-need-provenance-trailer\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - EslaMx7/ai-trailers: Capture AI coding tool prompts as standard git trailers in commit messages. Supports Claude Code, Kiro, Gemini, and Codex. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/EslaMx7/ai-trailers\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - theorib/git-commit-message-ai-prompt: An AI-assisted git Conventional Commit message prompt. Based on the Conventional Commits 1.0.0 Specification. Currently using the AI Commit VS Code plugin. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/theorib/git-commit-message-ai-prompt\"},{\"title\":\"Lore: Repurposing Git Commit Messages as a Structured Knowledge Protocol for AI Coding Agents\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2603.15566v1\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - josefaexistential382/ai-trailers: Track AI coding prompts as git trailers in commit messages to preserve intent in git history · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/josefaexistential382/ai-trailers\"},{\"title\":\"Assisted-by: How open source projects are drawing the line on AI contributions | We Love Open Source • All Things Open\",\"url\":\"https://allthingsopen.org/articles/open-source-ai-contributions-assisted-by-git-trailer-standard\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - QAInsights/code-provenance: Git Blame for AI-Generated Code · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/QAInsights/code-provenance\"},{\"title\":\"Stop Losing AI Context! Git AI Tracks Every Generated Line\",\"url\":\"http://prompts.brightcoding.dev/blog/stop-losing-ai-context-git-ai-tracks-every-generated-line\"},{\"title\":\"AI generated git commit messages - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/bdougieyo/ai-generated-git-commit-messages-4j7g\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Nutlope/aicommits: A CLI that writes your git commit messages for you with AI · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/nutlope/aicommits\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key findings about AI conversation provenance in git commits:\n\n## Git Trailers for AI Provenance\n\nGit trailers, sometimes called commit footers, are a long-established convention for adding structured metadata at the end of a commit message. This mechanism has become central to tracking AI-assisted development.\n\n## Three Primary Trailer Types\n\nThe open source community has converged on three main trailers for AI assistance:\n\n1. **Co-authored-by**: Designed for human collaborators; GitHub Copilot, Cursor, and Claude all default to emitting this trailer.\n\n2. **Generated-by**: Useful for the narrow case in which a tool produced essentially all of the content with minimal human revision, but misleading for the much more common case in which a developer prompts, reviews, edits, tests, and ships.\n\n3. **Assisted-by**: Indicates that an AI tool helped a human developer write or improve a contribution; the human committer is the author, the AI Agent and LLM are the tools, and this trailer matches the reality of most modern AI-assisted development.\n\n## Practical Implementations\n\nTools like ai-trailers capture AI coding tool prompts as git trailers in commit messages. Lore uses git trailers—a native git feature for structured key-value data in commit messages; no custom parsing is required, the format is queryable through standard git commands (git log --trailer=), and it is supported by every git tool in existence.\n\nThe projects in the middle that allow AI assistance with rules are quietly converging on a single piece of git metadata: a commit trailer called Assisted-by:, which is the most important standard that almost nobody is talking about.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PKAcNzz7ff