{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Do web research on existing tools/projects that capture, store, share, or replay AI coding-assistant conversation history, and report a competitive landscape summary.\n\nContext: I'm building \"conversation-ledger\": a small standalone unix-style utility that stores normalized, provenance-preserving records of visible coding-agent conversations (Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, aider, etc.) in a git repository — likely as content-addressed blobs referenced from a dedicated git ref like refs/notes/conversation-ledger, so the working tree and git status stay clean. Append-only evidence events with repo/branch/commit/author provenance, redaction via new manifests, explicit opt-in push/fetch. NOT an intent-analysis, summarization, or memory product — just durable attributable storage.\n\nSearch for (at minimum):\n- SpecStory (specstory.com) — captures Cursor/Claude Code chat history\n- aider's --chat-history / .aider.chat.history.md approach\n- Claude Code's own ~/.claude/projects transcript JSONL storage and /resume; claude-code session sharing tools on GitHub\n- Tools storing data in git-notes or hidden git refs (e.g. git-bug, git-appraise, gerrit's NoteDb) as architectural prior art\n- Any \"AI conversation provenance\", \"prompt provenance\", \"chat transcript in git\", \"commit-linked AI conversation\" projects on GitHub or HN\n- \"Continue.dev\" dev data, Cursor shared chats, OpenAI Codex CLI session storage\n- Anything doing commit ↔ conversation linking (e.g. tools embedding conversation IDs in commit trailers)\n\nFor each relevant find: name, one-line description, how it stores data, whether it links conversations to commits, and how it differs from conversation-ledger. End with: (a) the 3 closest competitors, (b) gaps nobody covers that conversation-ledger would, (c) any evidence this exact idea already exists. Keep the whole report under ~900 words.","type":"text"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"ma